   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Neuromuscular Adaptations Following Eight Weeks of French Contrast Training in Elite Youth Football Players: Implications for Athletic Performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Neuromuscular Adaptations Following Eight Weeks of French Contrast Training in Elite Youth Football Players: Implications for Athletic Performance</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>[-15]French contrast training; integrated training; plyometric training; neuromuscular adaptation; reactive strength; explosive performance; sprint acceleration; football; anaerobic power; force-vector specificity</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Neuromuscular training is increasingly recognised for enhancing football performance and athletesâ€™ capacity to tolerate the repetitive high-intensity demands of match play. The French Contrast Method (FCM) combines heavy resistance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Neuromuscular training is increasingly recognised for enhancing football performance and athletesâ€™ capacity to tolerate the repetitive high-intensity demands of match play. The French Contrast Method (FCM) combines heavy resistance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">plyometric</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">plyometric</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and ballistic exercises within a post-activation performance enhancement framework</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and ballistic exercises within a post-activation performance enhancement framework</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">yet evidence in elite youth players during the competitive season remains limited. This study examined the effects of an eight-week in-season FCM program on reactive strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">yet evidence in elite youth players during the competitive season remains limited. This study examined the effects of an eight-week in-season FCM program on reactive strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">neuromuscular performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">neuromuscular performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">maximal strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">maximal strength</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">anaerobic performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">anaerobic performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and short-distance sprint ability. Twenty-eight elite male youth football players were randomly assigned to an experimental group (FCM plus regular football training; n = 14) or a control group (regular training only; n = 14)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and short-distance sprint ability. Twenty-eight elite male youth football players were randomly assigned to an experimental group (FCM plus regular football training; n = 14) or a control group (regular training only; n = 14)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">training twice weekly for eight weeks. Outcome measures included the Reactive Strength Index Modified (RSImod)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">training twice weekly for eight weeks. Outcome measures included the Reactive Strength Index Modified (RSImod)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">countermovement jump-derived variables</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">countermovement jump-derived variables</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">10 m sprint performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">10 m sprint performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Wingate anaerobic parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Wingate anaerobic parameters</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and one-repetition maximum (1RM) strength. Data were analysed using mixed-design repeated-measures ANOVA. Significant group $\times $ time interactions favoured the FCM group for RSImod</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and one-repetition maximum (1RM) strength. Data were analysed using mixed-design repeated-measures ANOVA. Significant group $\times $ time interactions favoured the FCM group for RSImod</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">back squat 1RM</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">back squat 1RM</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">propulsive phase duration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">propulsive phase duration</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">average power</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">average power</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and fatigue index (p \textless  0.05)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and fatigue index (p \textless  0.05)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whereas no interactions emerged for 10 m sprint</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whereas no interactions emerged for 10 m sprint</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">flight time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">flight time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">jump height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">jump height</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">or bench press 1RM. The findings indicate that adding an eight-week FCM training programme to regular soccer training results in changes to specific performance outcomes in elite young soccer players. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">or bench press 1RM. The findings indicate that adding an eight-week FCM training programme to regular soccer training results in changes to specific performance outcomes in elite young soccer players. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">because direct neuromuscular measures were not assessed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">because direct neuromuscular measures were not assessed</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the underlying mechanisms responsible for these changes cannot be confirmed. Therefore</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the underlying mechanisms responsible for these changes cannot be confirmed. Therefore</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the results should be interpreted in terms of the observed performance outcomes rather than as direct evidence of neuromuscular adaptations.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the results should be interpreted in terms of the observed performance outcomes rather than as direct evidence of neuromuscular adaptations.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
